plasm (B3) while strongly supporting lymphoid neoplasm (B2) due to LR- exclusion of reactive processes."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) and blast morphology in peripheral blood support a lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorder despite missing specific features?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms with blasts provide weak evidence for lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders (B4) but do not strongly support this diagnosis due to missing monoclonal features."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of TdT positivity or B-cell immunophenotyping in the vignette sufficient to argue against lymphoid neoplasm (e.g., ALL) as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Lack of B-cell markers (TdT, immunophenotype) provides neutral evidence for lymphoid neoplasm (B2) as blast morphology alone cannot distinguish ALL from other processes without further immunophenotyping data."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset over days with 35% blasts and pancytopenia contradict chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (e.g., CML) due to the absence of chronic phase features (splenomegaly, basophilia)?", "summary": "The subacute presentation with high blast count (35%) moderately disfavors chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3) as this suggests blast crisis rather than chronic phase CML."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of monoclonal gammopathy (e.g., serum M spike) or lytic bone lesions in the vignette definitively rule out multiple myeloma as a diagnostic possibility?", "summary": "The absence of monoclonal features and bone lesions provides neutral evidence for lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders (B4), as these findings are not pathognomonic for multiple myeloma."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.225], ["B4", 0.193], ["B4.1", 0.116]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.52], ["B3", 0.338], ["B4", 0.137]]